### Longest Palindromic Substring

**Problem:** Given a string, find the longest substring that reads the same forward and backward.

**Approach:**
- Use the Expand Around Center technique.
- Consider every character as a possible center of a palindrome.
- Expand in both directions while the characters on the left and right are equal.
- Check both odd-length palindromes (single character as center) and even-length palindromes (two adjacent characters as center).
- Track the starting index and maximum length of the longest palindrome found.
- Extract and return the longest palindromic substring.

**Time Complexity:** O(n²) – For each character, we expand around its center.

**Space Complexity:** O(1) – Only a constant amount of extra space is used, excluding the output string.

**Concepts Used:**
- String Traversal
- Two Pointers
- Expand Around Center
- Palindrome Checking
- Substring Manipulation

###Solution

class Solution {
    public String longestPalindrome(String s) {
        if (s == null || s.length() < 1) return "";
        
        int start = 0;
        int end = 0;
        
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            // Case 1: Odd-length palindromes (e.g., "racecar", center is 'e')
            int len1 = expandAroundCenter(s, i, i);
            
            // Case 2: Even-length palindromes (e.g., "aabbaa", center is between 'b' and 'b')
            int len2 = expandAroundCenter(s, i, i + 1);
            
            // Get the maximum length found at the current center
            int len = Math.max(len1, len2);
            
            // If we found a longer palindrome, update our tracking boundaries
            if (len > end - start) {
                start = i - (len - 1) / 2;
                end = i + len / 2;
            }
        }
        
        // end + 1 because the second argument of substring() is exclusive in Java
        return s.substring(start, end + 1);
    }
    
    private int expandAroundCenter(String s, int left, int right) {
        // Expand outwards as long as characters match and indices are in bounds
        while (left >= 0 && right < s.length() && s.charAt(left) == s.charAt(right)) {
            left--;
            right++;
        }
        // Returns the length of the valid palindrome found
        return right - left - 1;
    }
}
